2. THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL AND ATTRIBUTION
---------------------------------------
Parts of the knowledge packs and skills in this repository adapt publicly
documented frameworks, specifications, standards and open-source projects.

Attribution is carried IN THE FILE THAT DOES THE ADAPTING, not in a central
list. The convention, which `rules/source-attribution.md` states in full, is
that a pack adapting a named external source carries a block giving the source,
the source's licence, and what was changed:

    **Adapted from**: <source name + URL or citation>
    **Source licence**: <the source's licence>
    **V2V refinements**: <what this work added, changed or refined>

The convention ships in this repository at `rules/source-attribution.md`; the installer
also projects it to `.claude/rules/source-attribution.md` in the installed workspace.

Why a list is not printed here instead: a central roster of sources is a second
copy of information that already exists beside the content, and a second copy
drifts. The attribution block travels with the material it describes, so a pack
cannot be moved, forked or edited out of its own attribution.

Coverage of that convention is not yet complete, and this notice will not claim
otherwise. Some packs carry the block in full; some carry attribution in an
HTML comment or a footer line; some are original work that correctly carries
nothing. Before redistributing a modified pack, read the file itself rather
than assuming a block is present — and if you find adapted material with no
block, treat the in-body citations as the statement of source.

Some adapted sources carry share-alike terms (for example CC-BY-SA). Those
terms attach to the adapted material itself, not to this repository as a whole,
and they are stated in that material's own attribution block or its in-body
citation. Read the file before redistributing a modified pack.

Concepts in common general use — RICE, MoSCoW, RACI, OKRs, SWOT, kanban, "user
story", "stakeholder map" and the like — carry no attribution, being too widely
used to have an owner.


